<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Second Taifa of Málaga</h1><p class="st-meta">1073 CE – 1090 CE · capital Málaga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Málaga under the Zirid prince Tamim, who broke away from his brother's kingdom of Granada until the Almoravids removed both.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the Zirid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badis-ibn-habus">Badis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> died in 1073, his grandsons divided his realm. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamim-ibn-buluggin">Tamim ibn Buluggin</a> received Málaga and ruled it as an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a>, while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-buluggin">Abd Allah</a> held the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-granada">Taifa of Granada</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamim-ibn-buluggin">Tamim</a> governed a prosperous port city producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, ceramics and dried fruit. The brothers quarrelled and fought over border towns, and both paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a>. Tamim joined the Almoravid campaign of 1086 but, like other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kings, was distrusted by their emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1090 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> deposed both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamim-ibn-buluggin">Tamim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-buluggin">Abd Allah</a>. Tamim was exiled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Sus</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a>. Málaga became an Almoravid city and later re-emerged briefly as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/third-taifa-of-malaga">Third Taifa of Málaga</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1073 CE – 1090 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.05%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1073 CE – 1090 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Málaga</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tamim ibn Buluggin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zirid Málaga</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1082&amp;focus=second-taifa-of-malaga" aria-label="Second Taifa of Málaga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4569 854 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Second Taifa of Málaga lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4889 1110L4910 1095M4889 1110L4799 1289"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 1095h0M4799 1289h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4889 1110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4889 1110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Málaga. <a href="/atlas?year=1082&amp;focus=second-taifa-of-malaga">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
